.0M)"], "rationale": "The fundamentals favor Republicans, but the only high-quality recent poll gives Taylor a four-point lead, early voting reports point Democratic, and expert ratings have drifted toward Tossup. Markets still price Wittman slightly ahead, so the race sits near even with a modest Democratic edge.", "watch": "Whether the Democratic early-voting edge holds through Election Day, or Wittman's fundraising advantage and incumbency reassert themselves.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Rob Wittman (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Virginia's 1st District has become one of the cycle's most competitive House races. Rob Wittman, a well-funded incumbent in a district with a three-point Republican lean, faces Shannon Taylor in an environment rated strongly favorable to Democrats. A Washington Post/SSPG poll in late September showed Taylor leading by four points among likely voters, while a Democratic-sponsored poll had the race roughly even. Ratings are split: several outlets call it a Tossup, others Lean Republican, and one rates it a Democratic flip. Prediction markets still give Wittman a slight edge, and he holds a large cash-on-hand advantage. Headlines highlight Medicaid backlash against Wittman and early voting trends favoring Democrats. The race is genuinely close, with a small Democratic tilt in the most recent evidence, but Wittman's incumbency and money keep it well within reach for either side.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4506, "margin": -0.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4359, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4341, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4371, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.3778, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3736, "margin": -1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.3951, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.4096, "margin": -2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.4532, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "